A||||10||Coop88||Clutch life||||5||
Z||000000||Coop88||01-02-2001||12:43 AM||||I was wondering how long a clutch usually lasts. I have about 148k miles on mine. According to the previous owner, it's the original. I rarely drive it hard. I know it depends on my driving style, but am I on borrowed time here? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||01-02-2001||12:51 AM||||I replaced the factory clutch in my Formula the other year. It had over 150K miles on it, and was still working fine, but I figured I might as well replace it when I had the time before school started back up. Really depends the type of usage. City, highway, etc. I'd say you're nearing the end of it's usable life.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mshill||01-02-2001||05:09 PM||||My 86SE had 185K miles on it when it had to be replaced.  It was the original, but I believe the previous owner used it as a long commute car so the miles were mostly highway miles.  If you do a lot of stop and go city driving I have to believe it should last 90k miles like the one on my 86GT whose owner drove it hard and put it away wet (cold).||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bruce||01-02-2001||09:17 PM||||Then, I have a question. I had my clutch replaced 20,000 miles ago and I don't drive the car hard. Slave and master cylinder were replaced at same time, but the car seems to be "slipping" in early rpm stages of shifting. Doesn't do it all the time tho'.<BR>HELP!||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||01-02-2001||11:45 PM||||Do you mean the clutch is "softer" when releasing the clutch. My Formula has a RAM clutch set in it, and it doesn't lock down very quick durring hard shifting and acceleration. My '86 2.8 with 4-speed still has the factory clutch in it, and I think it has a higher clamping force, since that clutch grabs really well, no matter how hard I beat on it.<P>For a daily driver with no spirited driving, I would go for the RAM clutch set. Pedal feel is light, maybe too light, engagement is smooth, and feedback is good.<P>I have not tried the FieroShops clutch yet, but have read many positives about it. I don't know much about it, but the clutch disk looks awsome, and is supposed to last a long time. <p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fiero Fanatic||01-03-2001||12:12 PM||||If you do relace it. It is possible to perform the changing of the clutch, pressure plate, bearing, even the clutch fork (good idea). All without dropping the cradle or taking out the transmission.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bruce||01-03-2001||04:32 PM||||But, my question is: if my clutch only has 20,000 miles, where else should I be looking for slipping and stuttering symptoms?<BR>Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||01-03-2001||05:04 PM||||You didn't use ATF anywhere did you?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bruce||01-03-2001||08:44 PM||||Nope.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||01-03-2001||08:47 PM||||did you check the arm on the tranny? If the bearings on it are going it could bind, keeping the clutch from grabbing right.||||reg||1
